                <pb facs="0344" n="1802" xml:id="pg1802"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On September 8, 1951, the Treaty of Peace with Japan was signed at San Francisco
                    by representatives of the United States, 47 other Allied Powers, and Japan.
                    Neither the Republic of China nor the People’s Republic of China was a
                    participant. Chapter II, Article 2 (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) of the
                    treaty provided that Japan renounce “all right, title and claim to Formosa and
                    the Pescadores” without specifying any recipient of that territory. For the text
                    of the treaty, see 3 UST (pt. 3), 3169–3328; for related documentation, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1951v06p1/pg_777">volume VI, Part 1, pages 777</ref>
                    ff.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
